## Problem reports

{% if config.failureReports.enabled %}
When a download fails, your app can offer to send a problem report. Nothing is sent unless you choose to. A report contains the page address, technical details about the failure and your comment; you see what it contains before you send it. Administrators use reports to fix failing downloads. You can see and withdraw your reports at any time.
{% if config.failureReports.forwarding is not null %}
If you agree, the instance forwards your report to the FetchDock developers, under the privacy policy of the FetchDock report collector (version {{ config.failureReports.forwarding.collectorPolicyVersion }}).
{% else %}
Reports stay on this instance.
{% endif %}
{% else %}
This instance does not accept problem reports. Reports sent earlier are kept until you withdraw them or an administrator deletes them.
{% endif %}

## Integrity of the software

This policy describes what unmodified FetchDock {{ appVersion }} does. The instance regularly checks its application code against the files of the official build{% if config.integrityCheckOnline %} and compares them with the fingerprint (digest) the FetchDock project published for this version{% endif %}. The generated policy is only published while that check passes.

This is a signal, not proof: it catches accidental changes, but a deliberately modified server could fake it. Anyone can compare the instance's published manifest digest with the one attached to the FetchDock release. The check does not cover the admin web app, the operating system image, or the downloader tools (yt-dlp and gallery-dl update themselves).

## Your rights

You have the right to access your data, to have it corrected or deleted, to restrict its processing, to receive it in a portable format and to object to its processing. Contact the operator to exercise these rights.

You also have the right to complain to a data protection supervisory authority{% if a.supervisoryAuthority is not null %}, such as {{ a.supervisoryAuthority }}{% endif %}.

